-- emit: the derived `eq` of a STRUCTURAL shape — a record, a tuple, `()`
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §9.2, §9.3, §8.5, A.46).
--
-- **The golden is §9's SHAPE, not its listing.** §9.4's and §9.6's worked
-- examples name the two operands `x` and `y` and the nominal function
-- `<Type>$eq`; the emitter writes `$x`, `$y` and `<Type>$$eq`, because a
-- derived body's operands share a namespace with the module's own locals
-- and the nominal base has to be one no module path can spell (§8.5). The
-- structure — which positions are compared, in what order, and what a tag
-- test does — is the spec's; the spelling is this file's, and only the
-- spelling.
--
-- A shape has no owning module, so it is derived where it is USED and
-- keyed on the shape and nothing else: the field names sorted by text, or
-- the arity. One evidence parameter per position, in the same order,
-- applied to the two values position by position — which is why
-- `{ x : Int, y : Int }` and `{ x : String, y : Char }` are ONE
-- `eq$r$x$y` here and differ only in the two arguments each use hands it.
-- Baking the first requester's element targets into the function is the
-- miscompile A.46 records.
--
-- `t2` says the same for tuples: `( Int, Int )` and `( Char, String )`
-- share it. `eq$unit` takes no evidence and answers `true`, because `()` is
-- `null` at runtime and both operands hold the same value.
--
-- The functions come before every declaration and are sorted by printed
-- name text — `eq$prim`, `eq$r$x$y`, `eq$t2`, `eq$unit` — which is the pass
-- of §8.5, and none of them is exported: each consumer emits its own.
import Node exposing (Program)


pub samePoint : { x : Int, y : Int }, { x : Int, y : Int } → Bool
samePoint a b = a == b


pub sameLabel : { x : String, y : Char }, { x : String, y : Char } → Bool
sameLabel a b = a == b


pub samePair : Int × Int, Int × Int → Bool
samePair a b = a == b


pub sameMixed : Char × String, Char × String → Bool
sameMixed a b = a == b


pub sameUnit : ⊤, ⊤ → Bool
sameUnit a b = a == b


pub differs : { x : Int, y : Int }, { x : Int, y : Int } → Bool
differs a b = a ≠ b


main : Program
main = Node.printLines []
